      <div class="ldet glass rv d1" aria-live="polite">
        <div id="lp1"><span class="ln2">L1 · Everyday services</span><h3>The only layer you see</h3><p>Paying, flying, emailing. It works only if every layer beneath it works.</p><div class="ex"><b>Nov</b>A ground stop of under five hours cancelled flights for three days.</div></div>
        <div id="lp2" hidden><span class="ln2">L2 · Institutions</span><h3>Built on others’ systems</h3><p>Banks, airlines and governments run on technology they often don’t own.</p><div class="ex"><b>Nov</b>Microsoft’s outage cut online communications for businesses and governments for over 24 hours.</div></div>
        <div id="lp3" hidden><span class="ln2">L3 · Cloud &amp; software</span><h3>Few platforms, many users</h3><p>A handful of providers carry the email, files and logins of countless organisations.</p><div class="ex"><b>Nov</b>One company’s failure became a global outage.</div></div>
        <div id="lp4" hidden><span class="ln2">L4 · Networks &amp; hardware</span><h3>Small parts, big stops</h3><p>Routers, cables and servers move the data that everything above depends on.</p><div class="ex"><b>Nov</b>The FAA’s ground stop was blamed on a single router installation.</div></div>
        <div id="lp5" hidden><span class="ln2">L5 · People &amp; maintenance</span><h3>The layer holding it up</h3><p>Old systems need people who understand them, and time to patch them safely.</p><div class="ex"><b>Nov</b>Microsoft released fixes for nearly 1,100 vulnerabilities this month, almost a year’s worth, according to reports.</div></div>
