        <p><span class="tag t-h">Hypothetical</span> Illustrative scenarios, not real events.</p>
        <h4>Discrepancies and open questions</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Microsoft’s outage is described both as “server issues” and as a months-old bug with a cascading networking problem. These may describe different layers of the same incident. The reporting does not reconcile them.</li>
          <li>For banks, one account reports transaction-processing problems at Chase. Another reports online-account access problems while card payments appeared to work, and names Bank of America and Wells Fargo without dates or causes.</li>
          <li>The FAA cause is reported both as a router installation and as a router update.</li>
          <li>The NSA outage’s date is not established, so it sits outside the dated timeline. Causes and scale for the United and Apple disruptions were not reported.</li>
          <li>The “98% certainty” figure is an official’s spoken estimate, not a forensic finding. Statements that the outages were not cyberattacks are assessments, not published investigation results.</li>
          <li>The CISA figures are a proposed budget cut and reported staffing losses.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Revisions</h4>
        <p>Earlier versions included a 74-country reach for the Microsoft outage, effects on IndiGo and Akasa Air, and UK hospital disruption. These came from an earlier Network18 Creative piece, not from independent sources, and have been removed. That piece remains a design reference only.</p>
        <h4>Images and data</h4>
        <p>The opening glitch is a visual effect. It does not depict any real outage or a shared cause. The night map is an original graphic, not a satellite image. Land outlines come from Natural Earth (public domain), and lights mark cities of 60,000 or more from GeoNames (CC BY 4.0). The animated points are symbolic and do not mark real outages. The scenario illustrations are original. Fonts are Barlow Condensed, Inter and IBM Plex Mono (SIL Open Font License).</p>
